\f4\fs26 The Elvi were artists and scientists reborn, \cb2 eagerly brain-dumping to the limit\cb1 , gobbling down science texts and jamming their brains with libraries of information, \cb2 expanding their appreciation of the elegant complexity and unlikelihood of existence.\cb1  A nation of evolutionary abominations fearlessly jumping to the defence of Darwin. Their souls, filled with the joy of simply being alive, crooned the praises of reason to anyone who would listen, their songs as uplifting as they were solemn. \cb2 Sales continued to shoot upward, to heights previously unknown. Elvi concerts always sold out,\cb1  the message of The Dawkins and the imminent arrival of his replacement, the one-after-the-one, receiving wild applause and unquestioned acceptance. The ovation would always be accompanied by the crowd screaming in maniacal unison, \'93Dawks, Dawks, Dawks.\'94 \
The popularity of the Elvi drew no official attention from \cb2 the Directorate of Moral Observance for a very long time\cb1 . Conformity to the laws of business and adherence to the social hierarchy, ensured the distribution of wealth always flowed upward and the shit always flowed down. Respect was only permitted to look up and was always to be returned with scorn. \
At first glance, the Elvi served this purpose very well. Considered as only one entity and unable to claim individual royalties or accumulate sufficient wealth to wield any individual power, they were ripe for exploitation. Deprivation of economic advancement was usually sufficient to crush any fanciful dreams of social climbing and even if there were one hundred thousand of them jitter-bugging around the Dominion, no Elvis would wield power.\
Fans of the Elvi, \cb2 the Dawks\cb1 , saw themselves as a benign, loosely affiliated group of wildly enthusiastic music fans that, for the most part, did not really care about social change, the environment or some dude that might get born in the future. About the only seditious act they indulged in was pasting 
\f5\i \cb2 \'93The one-after-the-one is coming\'94
\f4\i0 \cb1  posters on any and every vertical surface that would readily accept glue. A few appeared on walls here and there, but before long, the posters became ubiquitous. \cb2 The Pale was transformed into a thicket of papier-m\'e2ch\'e9 monoliths\cb1 , gathered together in praise of The Dawkins and silently awaiting the one-after-the-one. Where most saw a collection of useless, drug fucked stoners, the \cb2 Moral Compliance Directorate \cb1 saw the Dawks representing doctrinal divergence of the worst kind, an organised threat to the natural order. \
The Elvi managed to fly under the radar of Moral Compliance until they spoke of the \'93One after the One\'94, the great leader to come, as the deliverer of hope. Moral Compliance cared not about silly songs righteously regaling the merits of saving trees and whales. \cb2 There were fuck-all trees and no whales left to save anyway. They could sing all they liked, it would amount to nothing. Prophesy was not something the company gave much credence, but the Elvi were preaching social change, equality and freedom. Giving hope to working class scum was something that would not be tolerated, the lows would not be permitted to taste of such rich fruit. The minority of the opulent must be protected from the majority. Radical and decisive action was needed to ensure they dined on ashes forever.\
\cb1  \
The CEO had not anticipated how dangerously contagious was the meme of hope, now threatening to spread throughout the dominion. There were, suddenly, so many Dawks that they had not the resources or the time to arrest and process what appeared to be about half the population. The infection was not just limited to the lows, management-class music platforms and vid-shows were replete with praise for the latest Elvis masterpiece, which were played ad infinitum. \cb2 Much to his chagrin, the CEO had found himself, on more than one occasion, humming an Elvis classic. \cb1 \
A meeting with the CEO was hastily arranged when the analysis from Moral Observance hit the in-boxes of all the Department Directors. The Elvi and their army of Dawks were challenging the power of the state and had to be stopped, but there was just so many of them. Damien saw the enormity of the task ahead, getting rid of one hundred thousand much loved entertainers, was not be something he would want appearing on his resume and hoped his department didn\'92t get the gig. The logistics of killing off all of the Dawks, far more numerous than the Elvi, did not bear thinking about.\
\'93Concentration Camps.\'94 The Director of Corrections immediately placed on the table. He never missed an opportunity to increase his budget. \
\'93We can\'92t imprison them all, we don\'92t have the resources. Unless your department can pay for all of it.\'94 Damien shot him down.\
The Director of Prisons coughed and blurted out, \'93Well, we would have to discuss the fiscal arrangements.\'94 He could feel his face going red.\
 \'93Killing them all is not an option either.\'94 He looked straight at the No 1 screw, knowing he had snuffed out his little bit for more power. \'93It\'92s a logistical nightmare and we still would not get them all. The cost would be astronomical.\'94 Damien knew his opposition to wholesale slaughter would be interpreted as moral weakness and had couched his argument in terms of economic burden. Fiscal responsibility was the only safe ground upon which to take a contrary position to prevailing wisdom. \
\'93We can round them all up at the World Elvis Convention, it\'92s being held here this year.\'94 The Director of Intelligence liked killing people, so popping off a bunch of clones would not raise any moral quandary. \
\'93There would be an outcry here and worldwide. The Elvi around the world number in their millions and they take care of their own.\'94 The Commissioner of Police did not want to see any social disquiet or an invasion of foreign Elvi for that matter. He wanted the chaos at the usual level of insanity. \
\'93There is a huge industry based around these clones. \cb2 Many L.5\'92s have invested heavily in Elvis stocks and they would be severely affected.\cb1 \'94 The Director of Economic Advancement cared little for the Elvi themselves, but he had the duty to ensure the money flowed, particularly when he was one of the many who had padded their portfolios with Elvis. \
\cb2 The CEO was deep in thought. 
\f5\i How do I eliminate all of the Elvi here and at the same time ensuring Foreign Elvi would be too scared to avenge their brothers? 
\f4\i0 \cb1 \
\'93What is your plan, sir?\'94 The bravest person in the room, the newly promoted \cb2 Damien\cb1 , was the first the actually ask what the CEO wanted. He hoped his question sounded as neutral as he had intended, but the CEO was notorious for exploding at the slightest breach in etiquette and Damien hunkered down for a good tongue lashing.\
\'93Everyone out!\'94 The CEO yelled. The Department Directors all stood and gently slid their chairs under the giant marble table. Each walked calmly toward the boardroom door, increasing their pace as the exit beckoned, breaking into a trot for the last two strides. Damien wanted to join the rats scurrying off the ship, but knew he had to stay and face the captain.\
\'93Thank fuck those idiots have gone. Do you want a drink?\'94 the CEO was all smiles. \'93You were the only one who would \cb2 dare speak his mind\cb1 . They just want to protect their own little fiefdoms. I despise their craven self-interest. All of them are too busy preserving their positions to actually do their jobs. How does Scotch sound?\'94\
\'93It\'92s only midday, sir. So yes, Scotch for me.\'94 Damien really had no clue what to expect from this guy, but he hoped a decent glass of scotch would allow him to regain some balance.\
The CEO made the order and an assistant appeared out of nowhere with two fat, chunky glasses of scotch, planting them silently on the table and in an instant, was gone. \
\'93\cb2 Tasmanian Scotch, the best there is.\cb1 \'94 The CEO raised his glass and clashed it into Damien\'92s, still untouched on the table. \'93Now, what we speak of now will can never speak of again.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir. Total quarantine of information.\'94 Damien sipped the whiskey and was over whelmed by the flavours exploding in his mouth. \'93Tasmanian certainly is the best, but how is it called Scotch?\'94\
\'93I own the naming rights.\'94 He thought that was all he needed to say to establish who possessed real power in this meeting and he did not want to bore Damien with the convoluted and literally murderous negotiations with the Scots. \cb2 \'93You are to form a team to dig down to Jamison Hardy\'92s abandoned Bio-Weapons research facility. If you don\'92t already know, the facility was involved in re-sequencing viral DNA to specifically attack certain genomes. Ultimately, it was hoped that the virus would be developed to the point that it would attack a single individual, leaving no identifiable cause of death. The perfect tool for assassination, a virus that could infect an entire civilisation and all but one survives.\'94\cb1 \
\'93I see sir, that is ingenious. The Elvi have exactly the same DNA, so if you can infect one you can infect them all. One infected Elvis at the Elvis Convention and they will be wiped out world-wide.\'94 Damien was sickened by the appalling scale of the genocide, but truly admired the audacity of the plan. \'93I\'92m in. How can I help?\'94 \
\'93\cb2 I will need samples of as many pathogens as you can recover from each of the seven decommissioned labs.\cb1  I will give you access to all the old archives, but they have not been formatted for brain-dump, so you will have to read it all. Most of it is science beyond the comprehension of you, or I for that matter. I will assign you an interpreter, an eminent Bio-engineer. He will point you in the right direction, but your first problem will be digging down to the laboratories. If you drill it out by hand you will need an army of agents and to maintain bio-security you cannot use explosives. A plasma cutter or thermal lances might suffice, but you will have to be very careful where you slice. Whatever plan you adopt, it will have to be done safely, quietly and quickly.\'94\
\'93Works of that magnitude could not help but draw attention. It is a tall order.\'94 \
\'93The other Director\'92s must be kept in the dark, if they get a sniff this, they will most certainly try steal the bio-agents themselves. There is a gold mine of research waiting to be dug up and they will want a piece of the action. Safely, quietly and quickly. We have to recover the pathogens, identify a suitable candidate and then re-sequence the genes to attack the Elvi genome. All this, before the World Elvi Convention in December, just eight months away.\'94\
\'93My operatives will have their contracts terminated at the conclusion of works. I will choose those who will not be missed. With your permission, there is one guy I really want to get on the team, Agent Jones. He really needs his contract terminated. Get this, he has officially changed his first name to Agent.\'94 Damien was nodding his head, \'93True story.\'94\uc0\u8232 \
\'93I find that really quite peculiar, that and the fact that you found it necessary to bring it to my attention.\'94 The CEO really hated it when underlings became relaxed and familiar enough to regale him with anecdotes from their pointless lives. \'93You can use and discard anyone you deem fit, just as long as they can keep a secret until the work is done.\'94 The CEO was grim. \'93The Elvi must to be wiped off the face of the earth.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri-5717\sl360\slmult1\sa160\qc\partightenfactor0

